---
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import Faq from '../components/Faq.astro';
import SwatchGrid from '../components/SwatchGrid.astro';
import { SEASONS, SEASON_ORDER } from '../lib/seasons-data';
import { SEASON_PAGES } from '../lib/season-pages';
import { UNDERTONE_LIST, undertonePalette } from '../lib/undertones';
import { UNDERTONE_PAGES } from '../lib/undertone-pages';

const groups = UNDERTONE_LIST.filter((group) => UNDERTONE_PAGES[group.id]);

const faq = [
  {
    question: 'What is a skin undertone?',
    answer:
      'It is the colour underneath your skin rather than on the surface of it. Surface colour changes with the weather, with sleep and with age; the undertone stays where it is. It is what decides whether gold or silver looks right on you, and whether cream beats optical white.',
  },
  {
    question: 'Is undertone the same as skin tone?',
    answer:
      'No, and mixing the two is the most common confusion here. Skin tone is how light or deep your skin is. Undertone is which way its colour leans — warm, cool or neutral. Every undertone occurs at every depth, so knowing one tells you nothing about the other.',
  },
  {
    question: 'How do I find my undertone at home?',
    answer:
      'Three checks, all by a window rather than under a bulb: hold gold against silver, hold an optical white shirt against a cream one, and look at the veins on the inside of your wrist. Two of the three agreeing is enough to settle it, and each undertone page below explains what its own result looks like.',
  },
  {
    question: 'What if the tests disagree with each other?',
    answer:
      'A split result usually means a neutral undertone, which is a real answer rather than a failed test. It is also worth repeating the checks with a clean face in daylight, since makeup and warm indoor lighting both push the result.',
  },
  {
    question: 'Does my undertone give me my colour season?',
    answer:
      'It gives you half of it. Undertone narrows four seasons to two; depth and contrast decide which of those two you are. The free colour report does the whole thing at once, from a photo or from seven questions you can answer at home.',
  },
];
---

<BaseLayout
  title="Skin Tone Colours: Find Your Undertone and the Shades That Suit It | colormatch"
  description="Which colors suit your skin tone. Find your undertone — warm, cool, neutral or olive — with three tests you can do at home, then see the shades that suit it with names and hex codes."
  path="/skin-tone-colors/"
  crumb="Skin tone"
  breadcrumbs={[{ name: 'Home', path: '/' }]}
  faq={faq}
>
  <section class="hero">
    <div class="shell hero__inner stack">
      <h1>Skin tone and the colours that suit it</h1>
      <p class="lede">
        Your undertone is the colour underneath your skin, and it decides more about what suits you than
        how light or deep your skin is.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section">
    <div class="shell stack-lg">
      <div class="narrow stack">
        <p>
          Two things get called skin tone and they are not the same. Depth is how light or dark your skin
          is. Undertone is which way its colour leans — gold and peach on the warm side, pink and blue on
          the cool side, or a mix of both. Depth is easy to see and tells you very little about colour.
          Undertone is harder to see and tells you nearly everything.
        </p>
        <p>
          That is why the advice to wear certain colours because you are fair, or to avoid others because
          you are dark, so often falls apart. Every undertone occurs at every depth. A very fair person can
          be warm and a deep-skinned person can be cool, and the shades that suit them follow the
          undertone rather than the depth.
        </p>
        <p>
          Below is each undertone with the colours that go with it. If you already know yours, go straight
          to it; if you do not, the three tests on any of those pages take about a minute.
        </p>
      </div>

      {
        groups.map((group) => (
          <div class="stack">
            <SwatchGrid
              title={`${group.name} undertone`}
              description={group.rule}
              swatches={undertonePalette(group).wear}
            />
            <p>
              <a href={`/${group.slug}/`}>Colours for a {group.name.toLowerCase()} skin tone</a> — the full
              page, with the tests and what to wear.
            </p>
          </div>
        ))
      }
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section section--tint">
    <div class="shell stack-lg">
      <div class="narrow stack">
        <h2>Undertone gets you halfway</h2>
        <p>
          Knowing you are warm narrows the four colour seasons to two, and knowing you are cool narrows
          them to the other two. What decides between the remaining pair is depth and contrast: whether the
          shades that suit you are light or deep, clear or softened. That is the part a photograph answers
          faster than a mirror does.
        </p>
        <ul class="tips">
          {
            SEASON_ORDER.map((id) => (
              <li>
                <a href={`/${SEASON_PAGES[id].slug}/`}>{SEASON_PAGES[id].heading}</a> —{' '}
                {SEASONS[id].tagline.toLowerCase()}.
              </li>
            ))
          }
        </ul>
        <p>
          <a class="btn btn--primary" href="/">Get your free colour report</a>
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section">
    <div class="shell stack-lg">
      <div class="narrow stack">
        <Faq title="Questions about skin tone and undertone" items={faq} />
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>
</BaseLayout>
